WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.156
Hi, I'm Noggin!

00:00:02.156 --> 00:00:05.621
This is a corpus of 3D programs, and a loop that writes,

00:00:05.621 --> 00:00:06.776
builds and checks them.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:13.387
Write the object as a program, build it, and measure the mesh.

00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:15.010
A measured gap caps the score.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.956
A planner turns the prompt into named parts, sized in metres.

00:00:24.376 --> 00:00:27.822
A coding agent writes one file per part, and the harness builds it.

00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:29.351
Gates measure the mesh.

00:00:29.351 --> 00:00:33.230
A floating part or an overlap comes back as a number and a fix.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.423
A judge model scores the renders.

00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:38.083
Then plain code applies the caps the gates earned.

00:00:38.503 --> 00:00:41.678
Each finding becomes a repair task, and the loop runs again.

00:00:41.678 --> 00:00:43.121
The best round is kept.

00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:46.107
Here is the formula.

00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:49.064
First, the judge's criteria, weighted.

00:00:49.641 --> 00:00:51.207
Minus a penalty per defect.

00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:53.307
Then a cap.

00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:57.103
A floating part caps the round at zero point six, however good it looks.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.388
One real run, a birdcage.

00:01:00.388 --> 00:01:03.608
In round zero the swing floats, and the score is capped.

00:01:04.028 --> 00:01:06.931
Round one hangs it on a chain, but a raised pole now

00:01:06.931 --> 00:01:07.899
hits the crane arm.

00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:11.998
Round two shortens the chain to fit the plan, and the swing floats again.

00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:18.011
Two million rows come from about three hundred and twenty five thousand ids.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:20.679
Each program repeats, once per caption and format.

00:01:22.092 --> 00:01:25.998
And a trap: over eight percent of the default split comes from

00:01:25.998 --> 00:01:27.951
benchmark generators the paper holds out.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.438
The judge decides how good the object looks.

00:01:31.438 --> 00:01:34.047
The ruler decides how good it is allowed to be.

00:01:37.192 --> 00:01:41.323
Programs you can build, gates that measure, and caps a judge cannot argue with.

00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:43.858
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:43.858 --> 00:01:44.448
I'm Noggin.

00:01:44.448 --> 00:01:44.743
Bye!
